(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-3-gram-negative-bacilli-quiz"]={"title":"Gram-Negative Bacilli — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which pair of toxins does enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli produce?","choices":["Coagulase and murine toxin","Heat-labile and heat-stable","Shiga toxin and endotoxin","Exotoxin A and exoenzyme S"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli makes a heat-labile toxin and a heat-stable toxin, which promote secretion of fluid and electrolytes by the intestinal epithelium. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 56, 17, 59 and 81"},{"q":"What is the reservoir of Escherichia coli O157:H7?","choices":["Pet turtles and reptiles","Rabbits and other mammals","The intestines of cattle","Wild rodents and fleas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the reservoir is the intestines of cattle; people are infected through contaminated beef, water and vegetables. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 60, 29, 76 and 83"},{"q":"In the scheme for Gram-negative pathogens, which genus is the obligate anaerobe?","choices":["Pseudomonas","Escherichia","Haemophilus","Bacteroides"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the obligate anaerobes branch of the Gram-negative rods holds Bacteroides; the aerobes include Pseudomonas, and the facultative anaerobes include Escherichia and Haemophilus. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, image on Slide 12"},{"q":"How does enteropathogenic Escherichia coli damage the intestine?","choices":["Secretes heat-stable toxin","Releases Shiga toxin into vessels","Adheres and destroys microvilli","Invades the villi of the colon"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the bacteria adhere to the intestinal lining and destroy the microvilli; they also inhibit the uptake of vitamin B1 (thiamin). — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 58, 56, 59 and 78"},{"q":"Which bacteria are actually more prevalent in the gut than Escherichia coli?","choices":["Klebsiella and Enterobacter","Proteus and Providencia","Salmonella and Shigella","Bacteroides and Bifidobacterium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Escherichia coli is the common aerobic, non-fastidious gut bacterium, but it is not the most prevalent; the anaerobic Bacteroides and Bifidobacterium are. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 55, 50, 51 and 69"},{"q":"What does the acellular pertussis vaccine in DTaP (diphtheria, tetanus and acellular pertussis) contain?","choices":["Toxoid and other antigens","Whole killed bacteria","Live attenuated bacteria","Ready-made antitoxin antibody"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: DTaP is an acellular vaccine containing toxoid and other antigens, given as a five-dose series between 2 months and 6 years. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the bubo of bubonic plague?","choices":["A gangrenous patch of skin","An ulcer at the bite site","A pus-filled lung cavity","A swollen, necrotic lymph node"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the bacillus multiplies in the flea bite, enters the lymph and causes necrosis and swelling of a node, the bubo, in the groin or axilla. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 88, 29 and 89"},{"q":"Which metabolic profile fits Pseudomonas?","choices":["Aerobic respiration, no fermentation","Ferments lactose, like coliforms","Obligate anaerobic fermentation","Ferments glucose, oxidase negative"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: pseudomonads use aerobic respiration and do not ferment carbohydrates; they produce oxidase and catalase. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 14 and 46"},{"q":"Which opportunist has multiple animal reservoirs and causes catheter-related urinary tract infections?","choices":["Providencia species","Pasteurella multocida","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Haemophilus ducreyi"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Providencia has multiple animal reservoirs and causes nosocomial infections, urinary tract infections with catheters, and gastroenteritis. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 72, 66, 96 and 100"},{"q":"Which traits do ALL members of the Enterobacteriaceae share?","choices":["Catalase negative, spore-forming","Ferment glucose, oxidase negative","Ferment lactose, oxidase positive","Obligate aerobes, oxidase positive"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: all enterics ferment glucose, reduce nitrates to nitrites, are oxidase negative and catalase positive; they are facultative anaerobes that grow best in air. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 46, 43 and 14"},{"q":"Which Escherichia coli pathotype produces Shiga toxin?","choices":["Enteropathogenic Escherichia coli","Enterohemorrhagic Escherichia coli","Enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli","Enteroinvasive Escherichia coli"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: enterohemorrhagic Escherichia coli O157:H7, also called Shiga toxin-producing Escherichia coli, carries Shiga toxin into host cells through a cell wall receptor that fuses with the host membrane. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 59, 56, 57 and 58"},{"q":"How is plague diagnosed?","choices":["An agglutination titer test","Black colonies on Rainbow agar","History, symptoms, bubo aspirate","Charcoal yeast extract culture"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: diagnosis depends on history, symptoms and laboratory findings from aspiration of the buboes, and it must be quick, because death can occur within 2 days of symptoms. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 90, 28, 41 and 64"},{"q":"How is Shigella transmitted?","choices":["By inhaling contaminated aerosols","By the bite of an infected flea","By animal bites and scratches","By swallowing contaminated food"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Shigella is transmitted by ingestion of fecally contaminated food; it then invades the villi of the large intestine. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 78, 30, 40, 83 and 96"},{"q":"Which pigment gives Pseudomonas aeruginosa its greenish-blue color?","choices":["Pyocyanin","Prodigiosin","Methylene blue","Crystal violet"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Pseudomonas aeruginosa produces the greenish-blue pigment pyocyanin, and it has a grapelike odor; many pseudomonads make water-soluble pigments. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 18, 14, 48 and 68"},{"q":"Which Bordetella pertussis adhesion protein, long a vaccine component, is missing from newly developing strains?","choices":["Pertactin","Exotoxin A","Porin OprH","Shiga toxin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: pertactin assists the adhesion of the pertussis bacterium to airway linings and has been a component of vaccines; pertactin-negative strains are developing, a possible connection to the rise of pertussis. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 37, 17, 20 and 59"},{"q":"Which form of vaccine is available for brucellosis?","choices":["A human acellular vaccine","An animal vaccine","A human subunit vaccine","A human oral vaccine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: an animal vaccine is available for brucellosis; the disease is also a potential bioweapon. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 28, 31, 74 and 98"},{"q":"Why can antibiotic therapy of a Gram-negative infection itself release endotoxin?","choices":["The drug switches on toxin genes","The drug activates the lipid A","Dying cells secrete it to defend","Killing the cells lyses them"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: endotoxin is part of the Gram-negative outer membrane and is released on the lysis of the cells, including lysis brought about by antibiotic therapy. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Who are the hosts of Salmonella typhi?","choices":["Humans exclusively","Poultry and their eggs","Reptiles such as turtles","Cattle and dairy herds"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: humans are the exclusive hosts of Salmonella typhi and Salmonella paratyphi, the most serious pathogens of the genus, causing typhoid and paratyphoid fever. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 73, 60, 76 and 77"},{"q":"Enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli is the leading cause of which illness?","choices":["Wasting infantile diarrhea","Hemolytic uremic syndrome","Bacillary dysentery","Traveler's diarrhea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli accounts for most cases of traveler's diarrhea: watery diarrhea, cramping and a low-grade to no fever. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 56, 58, 60 and 78"},{"q":"Which form of plague is highly contagious by aerosol?","choices":["Pneumonic","Bubonic","Septicemic","Sylvatic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: in pneumonic plague the infection is localized to the lungs, highly contagious by aerosol infection, and fatal without treatment. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 83 and 88 and image on Slide 84"},{"q":"Serratia marcescens pneumonia is typical in which hosts?","choices":["Contact lens wearers","Unvaccinated infants","Chronic alcoholics","Cystic fibrosis patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Serratia marcescens causes pneumonia in chronic alcoholics, as well as burn and wound infections, urinary tract infections, and septicemia and meningitis in immunocompromised hosts. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 68, 18, 19, 22 and 35"},{"q":"Which disease was Haemophilus influenzae once mistakenly thought to cause?","choices":["Pink eye","Chancroid","Influenza (the flu)","Whooping cough"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Haemophilus influenzae was misnamed as the causative agent of the flu; it causes acute bacterial meningitis, epiglottitis, otitis media, sinusitis, pneumonia and bronchitis. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 98, 30 and 100"},{"q":"Which pair of findings marks enteroinvasive Escherichia coli infection?","choices":["Rose spots and sustained fever","Bloody diarrhea and high fever","Watery diarrhea, little fever","Wasting diarrhea in infants"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: it causes profuse bloody diarrhea and high fever, from inflammatory disease of the large intestine; the very young and very old are most susceptible. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 57, 56, 58 and 74"},{"q":"Which mediator triggered by endotoxin acts as a vasodilator in shock?","choices":["Bradykinin","Fibrin","Collagenase","Colony stimulating factor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: endotoxin may trigger production of bradykinin, a vasodilator in shock, alongside its coagulation effects such as disseminated intravascular coagulation. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 9 and 10"},{"q":"Which people are most prone to Legionnaires disease?","choices":["Women with urinary catheters","Men over 50 and smokers","Contact lens wearers","Infants not yet vaccinated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: legionellosis is prevalent in males over 50 and in smokers, and as a nosocomial disease it affects elderly patients with diabetes, cancer, transplants, chronic alcoholism or chronic lung disease. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 40, 19, 35 and 65"},{"q":"Why might a Pseudomonas aeruginosa vaccine harm a patient with cystic fibrosis?","choices":["It causes the infection itself","It is delivered through the gut","It may worsen lung inflammation","No candidate vaccine exists"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: because patients with cystic fibrosis often have a chronic infection, vaccination may induce increased pulmonary inflammation and tissue damage. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which sources spread the animal salmonelloses?","choices":["Animal bites and scratches","Poultry, eggs and pet reptiles","Fleas from wild rodents","Aerosols from cooling towers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the animal salmonelloses are acquired by ingestion from cattle, poultry and eggs, rodents, reptiles (pet turtles, bearded dragons), animal and dairy products, and fomites contaminated with animal intestinal flora; backyard flocks are a source. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 76, 77, 40, 83 and 96"},{"q":"How does Yersinia pseudotuberculosis infection differ from Yersinia enterocolitica infection?","choices":["It forms buboes in the groin","It causes bloody dysentery","More lymph node inflammation","It is spread by fleas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Yersinia pseudotuberculosis causes an infection similar to Yersinia enterocolitica, with more lymph node inflammation; treatment is mostly supportive. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 80, 83, 88 and 78"},{"q":"Which part of the bowel does enteroinvasive Escherichia coli inflame?","choices":["The ileum","The large intestine","The small intestine","The gallbladder"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: enteroinvasive Escherichia coli causes an inflammatory disease of the large intestine, similar to shigellosis. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 57, 56, 73 and 80"},{"q":"Which organism causes melioidosis?","choices":["Burkholderia cepacia","Brucella abortus","Francisella tularensis","Burkholderia pseudomallei"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Burkholderia pseudomallei, from the soil and water of tropical climates, causes melioidosis: wound infections with nodules, pneumonia, septicemia, endotoxic shock and liver and brain abscesses. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 22, 23, 27 and 29"}]};
